                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "In the **GEO Stack**, *retrieval probability* is the likelihood that a piece of content is selected by an AI system during the **retrieval phase** and enters the candidate set for generation; if it is not retrieved, it cannot be cited.[1][3]\n\nThe GEO Lab describes it as the **foundational Layer 1** of the stack, before extraction, ranking, or citation can happen.[1][3] It is influenced by five main factors: **semantic alignment**, **entity match strength**, **structural clarity**, **topical isolation**, and **contextual reinforcement**.[1][3]\n\nIn practical terms, this means content is more likely to be retrieved when it:\n- closely matches the query\u2019s meaning,\n- names key entities clearly,\n- uses clean section structure,\n- stays focused on one topic per section,\n- and is reinforced by related content elsewhere on the site.[1][3]\n\nThe GEO Lab also distinguishes **retrieval rate** from **citation rate**: retrieval rate is how often content is fetched as a candidate, while citation rate is how often it appears in the final answer.[4]\n\n",
